  narrativeContent: `The Yukon rises in the lakes behind the Coast Mountains and runs 3,000 kilometres north and west to the Bering Sea. Its upper country belonged to the Tagish, the Southern and Northern Tutchone and the Hän; its northern tributaries, the Porcupine and the Peel, to the Gwich'in; its southeastern headwaters to the Kaska. Over the passes from the coast came the Tlingit, who carried trade goods inland and kept the trade to themselves. For all of them the river was the road.

## The company comes in the back door

The Hudson's Bay Company could not reach the Yukon from the sea; the Russians held the coast. So it came from the Mackenzie. Robert Campbell climbed from the Liard to Frances Lake in 1840, walked over to the Pelly, and in 1843 followed it down to a great river he called the Lewes. John Bell went from Fort McPherson on the Peel over the mountains to the Porcupine and down it in 1845 to the same river. In 1847 Alexander Hunter Murray built Fort Yukon where the Porcupine comes in, knowing it was on Russian ground, and in 1848 Campbell built Fort Selkirk where the Pelly comes in. The posts were always hungry. Supplies came thousands of kilometres by canoe and on men's backs, and the Chilkat Tlingit, who did not want the company in their trade, sacked Fort Selkirk in 1852. When the Americans bought Alaska they turned the company out of Fort Yukon in 1869.

## Surveyors and prospectors

The next people up the river were Americans looking for gold: Arthur Harper in 1873, Jack McQuesten and Al Mayo, who built the posts the miners lived from. In 1883 an army lieutenant, Frederick Schwatka, rafted the whole river from the lakes to the sea and named things as he went. In 1887 the Geological Survey sent George Dawson up the Stikine and down the Pelly, and William Ogilvie over the Chilkoot to fix the Alaska boundary on the river; Dawson said there would be gold. There was gold on the Fortymile in 1886, and the town of Forty Mile grew up around it with a bishop and, in 1895, twenty Mounted Police.

## Bonanza

On 16 August 1896 three men found gold on Rabbit Creek, a stream running into the Klondike: Keish, called Skookum Jim, his nephew Káa Goox, called Dawson Charlie, and George Carmack, the American married to Keish's sister Shaaw Tláa. Carmack staked the discovery claim and took the credit. The Tagish tell it differently: Keish and Charlie had come to find Kate, and the gold was almost an accident. Within a year the news reached Seattle and San Francisco, and a hundred thousand people started for the Klondike. Thirty thousand climbed the Chilkoot and White passes, built boats at Bennett Lake and, when the ice went out on 29 May 1898, floated down the river under Sam Steele's rules. Dawson, which Joseph Ladue had staked on the flats at the Klondike's mouth, had seventeen thousand people that summer.

## Whose river

The flats were the Hän's fishing camp, Tr'ochëk. Chief Isaac moved his people three miles down the river to Moosehide and spent the rest of his life reminding Dawson what it had cost them. Jim Boss of the Ta'an Kwäch'än, who had built roadhouses for the sternwheelers on Lake Laberge, made the first Yukon land claim in 1902. Keish's wealth broke his family, and Shaaw Tláa was left with nothing when Carmack left her. The gold rush lasted three years; the river and its people are still there, and the Tr'ondëk-Klondike landscape was named a World Heritage Site in 2023 for what the Hän did to survive it.`,
